## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level data structure design under bounded resources, including probing, memory layout, and edge handling (empty slots, full table).

## Key Concepts
- Open addressing with linear probing
- Fixed capacity and sentinel handling
- Separate arrays for keys and values for cache locality
- Return conventions for success/failure

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define N (1<<12)
static uint32_t keys[N];
static uint64_t vals[N];

static inline size_t probe(uint32_t k) { return k % N; }

void ht_init() {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) keys[i] = 0;
}

int ht_insert(uint32_t k, uint64_t v) {
  if (k == 0) return -1; // sentinel reserved
  size_t idx = probe(k);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (keys[idx] == 0) { keys[idx] = k; vals[idx] = v; return 0; }
    if (keys[idx] == k) { vals[idx] = v; return 0; }
    idx = (idx + 1) % N;
  }
  return -1; // full
}

int ht_lookup(uint32_t k, uint64_t* out) {
  if (k == 0) return -1;
  size_t idx = probe(k);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (keys[idx] == 0) return -1;
    if (keys[idx] == k) { *out = vals[idx]; return 0; }
    idx = (idx + 1) % N;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you resize or rehash when load factor grows? 
- How to detect and recover from clustering effects in probing? 
- What are alternatives to sentinel-based empties (bitmaps, tombstones)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests hands-on understanding of memory hierarchy, NUMA locality, and practical optimization trade-offs in a real kernel.

## Key Concepts

- Cache tiling for L1/L2 reuse
- NUMA first-touch locality on multi-socket systems
- Boundary handling for non-multiples of tile size
- Loop unrolling and prefetching strategies
- False-sharing avoidance via per-tile accumulation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-C kernel illustrating 32x32 tiling
void matmul_tiled(int N, const double *A, const double *B, double *C){
  for(int ii=0; ii<N; ii+=32){
    for(int jj=0; jj<N; jj+=32){
      for(int kk=0; kk<N; kk+=32){
        for(int i=ii; i< (ii+32) && i<N; ++i){
          for(int j=jj; j<(jj+32) && j<N; ++j){
            double sum = 0.0;
            for(int k=kk; k<(kk+32) && k<N; ++k){
              sum += A[i*N+k] * B[k*N+j];
            }
            C[i*N+j] += sum;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate correctness for non-multiple tile sizes?
- How would you adapt to AVX2/AVX-512 and data layout changes to boost throughput?

