## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical concurrency on bare-metal MCUs: interrupt-safe data sharing, wrap-around handling, and overflow behavior in a tight, deterministic path.

## Key Concepts
- Circular buffers with power-of-two sizing
- Volatile indices and memory visibility between ISR and main code
- Minimal interrupt masking to avoid latency spikes
- Edge-case testing for wrap and overflow

## Code Example
```c
#define N 32
#define MASK (N-1)
static uint8_t buf[N];
static volatile uint8_t head = 0; // consumer index
static volatile uint8_t tail = 0; // producer index

int enqueue_byte(uint8_t b) {
  uint8_t next = (tail + 1) & MASK;
  if (next == head) return -1; // full
  buf[tail] = b;
  // memory barrier if available
  tail = next;
  return 0;
}

int dequeue_byte(uint8_t *out) {
  if (head == tail) return -1; // empty
  *out = buf[head];
  head = (head + 1) & MASK;
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-producer/multi-consumer safely?
- What tests would you run on a simulated interrupt workload to prove correctness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tackles crash-consistency, non-volatile memory ordering, ABA avoidance, and safe memory reclamation in a high-concurrency data path.

## Key Concepts
- NVRAM durability and flush ordering (clwb/clflush, sfence)
- Per-slot sequence numbers to prevent ABA
- Epoch-based reclamation vs. hazard pointers
- Recovery: replay tail, prune uncommitted entries

## Code Example
```c
// simplified sketch showing write order and fences
void enqueue(Ring *r, void *payload){
  uint64_t t = fetch_and_add(&r->tail, 1);
  Slot *s = &r->slots[t & (N-1)];
  memcpy(s->payload, payload, 64);
  __sync_synchronize(); // fence before seq
  s->seq = t<<1 | 1; // mark committed
  // persist s and tail
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement recovery after power loss?
- How do you validate correctness under concurrent stress?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests crash‑consistency and lock‑free design under PMEM constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Crash‑consistency with persistent memory using CAS, fences, and transactional updates
- Per‑thread caches with a central allocator to reduce cross‑socket traffic
- Recovery and durability: reconstruct free lists from persistent root and/or logs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: pmem_alloc
function pmem_alloc() {
  if (!threadCache.empty()) return threadCache.pop();
  let batch = centralPool.acquireBatch();
  threadCache.fill(batch);
  return threadCache.pop();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-latency IPC in NUMA systems, with careful attention to ABA avoidance, memory ordering, and cache-line effects.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with per-slot sequence counters
- NUMA-aware memory layout and per-CPU ownership
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Hybrid wakeup (spin + eventfd/futex) to balance latency and CPU utilization

